{bloque === 'mantenimiento' && !en && (
  <section id="mantenimiento" class="seccion" aria-labelledby="h-mantenimiento">
    <h2 id="h-mantenimiento" class="font-display text-h2">Mantenimiento técnico</h2>
    <p class="cuerpo">Para los cambios que tocan la base de la web, para perfiles técnicos o para el propio Claude.</p>
    <dl class="dev-filas">
      <div><dt>Verificar</dt><dd><code>npx astro check</code>, <code>npx astro build</code>, <code>node tools/spacing-lint.cjs</code> y <code>node tools/verify.cjs &lt;url&gt;</code> en verde antes de dar algo por hecho, y otra vez en vivo después de publicar. Hoy <code>verify.cjs</code> carga Playwright desde una ruta del Mac de Andoni: en otro ordenador no arranca y hay que probar con un Playwright propio.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tokens</dt><dd>Color por estación, escala tipográfica y espaciado viven en <code>src/styles/global.css</code>. La estación de la web la lleva <code>src/scripts/estacion.ts</code> y se fija en el head (<code>Base.astro</code>) antes del primer fotograma.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Contextos WebGL</dt><dd>El roble, las raíces, el paisaje del pie y el Manifiesto los pinta el motor <code>src/scripts/estela.ts</code>, un lienzo WebGL cada uno. Chrome y Safari mantienen unos 16 a la vez y tiran los más viejos (la ronda 63 dejaba el hero en blanco). Por eso estela.ts DUERME los lienzos lejanos cuando hay más de <code>LIMITE</code> vivos (12 en Chrome; en Safari, siempre) y los recupera al acercarse. Antes de añadir otro lienzo, cuéntalos.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Fuentes</dt><dd>Autoalojadas con la API de fuentes de Astro (<code>astro.config.mjs</code>, archivos en <code>src/assets/fonts/</code>). Family es el archivo de PRUEBA de Klim: sin tildes ni ñ (caen a Georgia) y no se puede servir en abierto sin licencia: hay que comprarla. Timeless (Sans, Grotesk) va con la Timeless Free Font License 1.2 (<code>tools/fuentes-timeless/</code>): uso web y comercial libre, pero prohibido redistribuirla o subirla a un repositorio público. El nuestro es privado y así debe seguir.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>noindex</dt><dd>Todo se indexa. Una página que no deba salir en buscadores pasa <code>indexar</code> en <code>false</code> a <code>Base.astro</code>, como el Método y la 404. El sitemap lo genera <code>@astrojs/sitemap</code>.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Sonidos</dt><dd>La impresora, los ambientes y la música del reel son de Pixabay (uso libre sin atribución); origen, autor y tramo de cada uno en <code>tools/fuentes-sonido.md</code>. La radio, de HoliznaCC0 (dominio público), igualada a −18 LUFS. Nada que tenga «todos los derechos reservados».</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Copias de seguridad</dt><dd>Lo que se quita no se borra: va a <code>tools/retirados/</code> con la ronda en el nombre. Andoni guarda además una copia de la carpeta antes de cada ronda grande (<code>../savvia-web.bak-rNN-fecha/</code>, en su Mac). Y Git guarda cada commit.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Si Vercel bloquea un despliegue</dt><dd>Pasa cuando el commit es de alguien que no está en el equipo de Vercel de Andoni: queda en «Deployment was blocked» (ronda 83). Desde la ronda 174 la Action <code>publicar.yml</code> llama al Deploy Hook (secreto <code>VERCEL_DEPLOY_HOOK</code> del repositorio) y lo publica igual. Si aun así no sale: Andoni le da a Redeploy en Vercel o sube un commit suyo (vale uno vacío). Para que no vuelva a pasar, cada persona configura su identidad de Git con el correo de su GitHub.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>De emergencia</dt><dd>La receta antigua sigue valiendo: <code>astro build</code> y <code>vercel deploy --prod</code> desde <code>dist/</code>, con el proyecto <code>savvia-home</code>.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>
)}

{bloque === 'empezar' && en && (
  <Fragment>
    <section id="usar" class="seccion" aria-labelledby="h-usar">
      <h2 id="h-usar" class="font-display text-h2">How to use this system</h2>
      <p class="cuerpo">This page is the manual for the savvia website, and it comes from its own code: every colour, typeface or component you see here is the one you see on the website. It has three parts: how it looks (the brand), how it is made (the development) and this guide, which is the one you will use day to day to change the website with Claude.</p>
      <dl class="dev-filas">
        <div><dt>Working with the website</dt><dd>Receiving the project, working with Claude, changing content, adding sections and pages, and how to ask for changes. Start here.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>The brand</dt><dd>Logo, colour by season, typography, icons and motion. Check it to name a colour or a style when you ask for a change.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>The development</dt><dd>Stack, components, how each piece is made and the map of the website. It helps you point without doubt at what you want to touch.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Maintenance</dt><dd>Verifying, publishing, licences and what to do if something breaks. For technical profiles or for Claude itself.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <ul class="reglas">
        <li><b>Two documents rule</b> AGENTS.md (how we work, the dictionary, what was rejected and the log of every round) and DESIGN.md (Andoni’s design guide). If something here does not match them, they win.</li>
        <li><b>How we work</b> the Method lives separately and is coming soon.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="recibir" class="seccion" aria-labelledby="h-recibir">
      <h2 id="h-recibir" class="font-display text-h2">Receiving the project</h2>
      <p class="cuerpo">The whole website lives in a private GitHub repository: code, images, videos, fonts and documentation. This is done once per computer.</p>
      <ol class="pasos">
        <li>
          <h3 class="font-display text-h3">Ask for access to the repository</h3>
          <p>It is at <code>github.com/AndoniMart/savvia-web</code> and it is private: it holds licensed fonts and screenshots of client websites. You need a GitHub account and Andoni has to invite you as a collaborator.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 class="font-display text-h3">Bring it to your computer</h3>
          <p>With Ship Studio, “Import project” and choose the repository. With the terminal:</p>
          <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/AndoniMart/savvia-web.git</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 class="font-display text-h3">Install what is needed</h3>
          <p>You need Node 22.12 or later (<code>package.json</code> asks for it). Inside the folder:</p>
          <pre><code>npm install</code></pre>
          <p>The dependencies do not travel in the repository: they are rebuilt here.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 class="font-display text-h3">Start the website locally</h3>
          <p>The development server runs in the background, so it does not take up the terminal:</p>
          <pre><code>astro dev --background
astro dev status
astro dev logs
astro dev stop</code></pre>
          <p>Open the address it gives you and you will see every change live before publishing it. Locally, Agentation is always on, so you can leave notes pinned to each element.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 class="font-display text-h3">Set up your Git identity</h3>
          <p>With the email of your GitHub account. Without it, your commits go out under the name of the computer and Vercel does not know whose they are (round 83).</p>
          <pre><code>git config user.name "Your name"
git config user.email "your-github-email"</code></pre>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="conectar" class="seccion" aria-labelledby="h-conectar">
      <h2 id="h-conectar" class="font-display text-h2">Working with Claude</h2>
      <p class="cuerpo">The website is maintained by talking to Claude: you ask for the change in plain words and it touches the code. Eric and Andoni work on the same repository, each on their own computer, and Git does not sync by itself: there is a routine.</p>
      <ol class="pasos">
        <li>
          <h3 class="font-display text-h3">Open the project</h3>
          <p>In Ship Studio (Claude and the preview come already connected) or with Claude Code in the terminal, on the folder. When it opens, Claude reads CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md: it already knows the stack, the colours, the dictionary and what was rejected. There is no need to explain it.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 class="font-display text-h3">When you start: git pull</h3>
          <p>The hook <code>.claude/hooks/al-empezar.sh</code> does it by itself when each session opens: it pulls the latest from GitHub and tells you if there are commits or changes waiting to be pushed. If it warns that it could not (conflicting changes), that is solved before touching anything.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 class="font-display text-h3">Ask for the change and look at it</h3>
          <p>What you want, where and how it should end up. Check it in the preview, on desktop and on mobile.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 class="font-display text-h3">Keep it written down</h3>
          <p>Every round is noted in the log in AGENTS.md: what was asked (word for word), what was done, what was measured and what is pending. Whatever is discarded goes to “Rejected” and does not come back.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3 class="font-display text-h3">When you finish: commit and push</h3>
          <p>So the other person has it. Careful: a push to <code>main</code> PUBLISHES to savvia.studio. If the push is Andoni’s, Vercel publishes it by itself; if it is someone else’s, the “Publicar en Vercel” Action (<code>.github/workflows/publicar.yml</code>) calls the Deploy Hook. In a few minutes it is live. You push when whoever is working asks for it.</p>
          <pre><code>git add -A
git commit -m "Round NN: what changes"
git push</code></pre>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="contenido" class="seccion" aria-labelledby="h-contenido">
      <h2 id="h-contenido" class="font-display text-h2">Changing content</h2>
      <p class="cuerpo">The copy is not scattered across the components: it lives in a few data files. Changing a sentence means changing a line.</p>
      <h3 class="font-display text-h3 sub">Copy</h3>
      <dl class="dev-filas">{TEXTOS_EN.map(([t, d]) => (<div><dt><code>{t}</code></dt><dd>{d}</dd></div>))}</dl>
      <ul class="reglas">
        <li><b>Dictionary</b> “savvia” always in lower case, even at the start of a sentence. We speak to whoever arrives (you); the first person, only in the Studio. No italics.</li>
